  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An increase in deep layer moisture combined with a \nseries of disturbances aloft and eventually a cold front will \nresult in an increasing chance of showers for late Wednesday \ninto Thursday that maximizes on Friday. \n\nA rather active mid and upper level pattern across the CONUS for the \nrest of the work week features a weakening short wave trough that \nlifts across the Deep South into the southern Appalachians on \nWednesday night with trailing energy that moves across NC early\nThursday. A stronger short wave moves across the OH Valley on \nFriday and then into the mid-Atlantic on Friday night. While the\nbest forcing for ascent tracks north of the Carolinas, ascent \nwill be sufficient and supplemental by an advancing cold front \nthe briefly stalls across the western Carolinas on Thursday \nnight and is then nudged eastward by the mid/upper trough with\nthe front crossing the Carolinas on Friday afternoon and Friday\nnight. It's worth highlighting that precipitable water values \nacross central NC range between 1.5 and 2.0 inches which is from\n225 to 250% of normal! There is the potential that rainfall \namounts may be enhanced by convection although limited \ninstability and modest lapse rates will likely preclude the \nability of deep convection to utilize the 30-60 kts of deep \nshear for severe storms. \n\nDespite all of the ingredients for rain, the main players in this \nevent have evolved considerably as the event has drawn closer \nand the forecast still contains a greater than typical amount \nof uncertainty. Rainfall totals from Wednesday night through \nFriday night in the quarter to half inch range appear \nreasonable. As noted in the previous discussion, the possible \nranges of QPF are large. for instance the latest EC ENS ensemble\nhas the 10th percentile ranging from 0.10-0.25\" and the 90th \nranging from to 1-1.5 inches. There is growing confidence that \na very large portion of this rainfall will occur on Friday, \nespecially Friday afternoon across eastern areas. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Well above normal temperatures on Wednesday \nand especially Thursday with muggy conditions will fall to near\nlevels seasonable by Sunday and Monday. \n\nA warm airmass will spread into central NC this work week with low \nlevel thickness values climbing into the 1380s m on Thursday \nmorning. This will support highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s, \nwarmest on Thursday when highs will be 13 to 17 degrees above \naverage. Clouds and a southerly flow on Wednesday and Thursday night \nwill result in a warm and muggy night. A common pattern recognition \ntechnique is that the night before a cold frontal passage is \noften quite warm given the clouds and nocturnal southerly wind.\nLows on Friday morning should range in the lower and mid 60s \nwhich is 20 to 26 degrees above average!\n\nTemperatures will cool down to near average early next week \nwith highs in the lower to mid 60s for Sunday and Monday.",
